Apple's AirPods with cameras could improve spatial audio on the iPhone and other Apple devices by detecting the position of the display the wearer is looking at. Details on the capability were found in <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macos-26/">macOS Tahoe</a> 26.7 code, and <a href="https://forums.macrumors.com/threads/upcoming-airpods-may-use-cameras-for-improved-spatial-audio-head-tracking.2488206/">shared on the MacRumors forums</a> by member Mactracker.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/06/AirPods-Pro-4-With-Camera-Thumb.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1060535" /><br/>
Sensors in the current AirPods power a spatial audio head tracking feature, and cameras could take that further. Code suggests the camera-equipped AirPods will find the physical screen the wearer is watching content on, comparing the screen's direction with the direction estimated by the motion sensors in the AirPods.<br/>
<br/>
The AirPods may be able to identify laptops, displays, smartphones, tablets, and TVs, even detecting multiple displays and narrowing audio to the one the wearer is looking at. Screen detection will work behind the scenes and may pause when the wearer is in motion.<br/>
<br/>
Devices with <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/mask-face-id/">Face ID</a> already use the &zwnj;Face ID&zwnj; sensors to assist with head tracking, but if the user also has AirPods with cameras, the AirPods will prefer camera input over &zwnj;Face ID&zwnj;.<br/>
<br/>
Code also references a server-based visual reasoning feature, further reinforcing rumors of <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/visual-intelligence/">Visual Intelligence</a> support. The AirPods will reportedly send camera data to Apple's servers for &zwnj;Visual Intelligence&zwnj; requests.<br/>
<br/>
